---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import RerankDemo from "../../components/RerankDemo.astro";

const title = "What a cross-encoder adds";
const description =
  "Notes on cross-encoders: how they differ from embedding search and from generative LLMs, and where a reranker fits in a RAG pipeline.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} ogType="article">
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-10 md:pt-14 pb-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-muted">
      <a class="hover:text-text" href="/#essay">← Back</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="mt-8">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-muted"><span class="text-explore">Notes</span> · November 2026 · 5 min read</div>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[30px] md:text-[40px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.01em]">
        {title}
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed">
        I've built retrieval on embeddings for a while. This week I finally sat down with cross-encoders, the models
        that rerank results before an LLM sees them. These are my notes on what they add and when they're worth it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="mt-10 space-y-9 leading-relaxed">
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[22px]">Where I started</h2>
        <p>
          Most RAG systems start the same way. Embed every chunk, embed the query, return the nearest neighbors. In
          practice that's often pgvector with an embedding model like Cohere's. It's fast and it works.
        </p>
        <p>
          But nearest is not the same as relevant. A chunk can sit right next to the query in vector space and still
          not answer it. A cross-encoder is the piece built to fix that ranking, and I wanted to understand why it's
          better at it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[22px]">Bi-encoder: two separate passes</h2>
        <p>
          An embedding model is a bi-encoder. The query goes through the model on its own and becomes a vector. Each
          document goes through on its own and becomes a vector. You compare them with a dot product or cosine
          similarity.
        </p>
        <p>
          The big win is that documents are encoded once, ahead of time. At query time you encode one short string and
          run a nearest-neighbor search, so searching millions of chunks is cheap.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cost is that the model never sees the query and the document together. Everything it knows about a
          document has to fit in one vector, written before anyone asked a question.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[22px]">Cross-encoder: one pass over both</h2>
        <p>
          A cross-encoder takes the query and one document as a single input, roughly <code class="font-mono text-[14px] text-text">[CLS] query [SEP] document [SEP]</code>, runs it through a
          transformer, and outputs one number: how relevant this document is to this query.
        </p>
        <p>
          The part that clicked for me was the <code class="font-mono text-[14px] text-text">[CLS]</code> token. It's an
          extra token added at the very start, and it doesn't stand for any word. Because attention runs <em>across</em> the pair, every query token can look at every document token in every layer, and <code class="font-mono text-[14px] text-text">[CLS]</code> can look at all of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          On its own that token means nothing. Fine-tuning is what gives it a job. The model is trained on query and
          passage pairs labeled relevant or not, with a small layer on top that turns the final <code class="font-mono text-[14px] text-text">[CLS]</code> vector into one score. To get that score right,
          the model learns to gather everything that matters about the match into <code class="font-mono text-[14px] text-text">[CLS]</code>: a "not", a date that doesn't line up, or the one
          clause that answers the exact question.
        </p>
        <p>
          The catch: nothing can be computed ahead of time. Every query and document pair is a full forward pass. Run
          it over a million chunks and that's a million passes for one question. So a cross-encoder is a reranker, not
          a search engine.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here's the difference on a few kitchen tips. These are real scores from two small open models, one of
          each kind. You can also edit the query and run both models live in your browser.
        </p>
        <RerankDemo />
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[22px]">How it differs from an LLM</h2>
        <p>
          A cross-encoder and a model like GPT or Claude are both transformers, but they're built for different jobs.
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-[0.7em] size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-muted" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span>
              <b class="text-text font-medium">Shape.</b> A generative LLM is usually decoder-only. It reads left to right
              and predicts the next token. A cross-encoder is usually encoder-only, BERT style, so every token sees the
              whole input at once.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-[0.7em] size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-muted" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span>
              <b class="text-text font-medium">Output.</b> An LLM writes text. A cross-encoder writes one score. No
              parsing, no prompt, no chance of it explaining itself instead of answering.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-[0.7em] size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-muted" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span>
              <b class="text-text font-medium">Training.</b> It's fine-tuned on labeled query and passage pairs, like
              MS MARCO, so the only thing it learns to put into that one score is relevance.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-[0.7em] size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-muted" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span>
              <b class="text-text font-medium">Cost.</b> You can ask an LLM to rerank, and it works, but it's slower
              and pricier per pair. A small cross-encoder like MiniLM is tiny next to an LLM and can run on a CPU.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The way I hold it now: embedding models and cross-encoders differ in <em>where</em> the query and document
          meet. In a bi-encoder they meet at the very end, in one dot product. In a cross-encoder they meet at the
          start and interact in every layer.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[22px]">Where it fits: retrieve, then rerank</h2>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5 marker:text-muted">
          <li>Embedding search (and often keyword search too) pulls the top 50 to 100 candidates. Cheap, wide net.</li>
          <li>The cross-encoder scores each candidate against the query and keeps the best 5 or so. Slower, but only 100 pairs.</li>
          <li>The LLM answers from those few chunks.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Each stage costs more per item than the one before it, so each one sees fewer items.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[22px]">When I'd reach for one</h2>
        <p>
          It's worth it when the wrong chunk is expensive and the answer hinges on details. At TCS I built a RAG tool
          that pre-audits insurance claims against policy documents. A clause that's almost right is the worst kind of
          wrong there, and that's exactly the case a reranker is made for.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'd skip it when the latency budget is tight, when the corpus is small enough to hand the LLM everything, or
          when evals show the top results are already right.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first thing I'd measure is whether the right chunk shows up in the top 50 at all. If it doesn't, a
          reranker can't save it. I've seen English queries miss Telugu documents entirely, and the fix there was a
          better multilingual embedding model, not reranking. Reranking only helps once retrieval finds the right
          neighborhood.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-text text-[22px]">The short version</h2>
        <p class="text-text">Embeddings decide what's worth reading. The cross-encoder actually reads it.</p>
      </section>
    </article>

    <nav class="mt-14 pt-6 border-t border-grid text-sm">
      <a class="text-muted hover:text-text" href="/">← Home</a>
    </nav>
  </main>
</Layout>
